{#-
  RFC-0090 / phase-429 W1 — the CODEGEN VERSION STAMP emitted into every
  generated C and C++ header. This block is a jinja comment: it explains the
  mechanism to whoever edits the pack and does NOT land in the artifact, which
  keeps a long rationale out of every generated file in a user's tree.

  MECHANISM. The artifact declares the version it was emitted at and compares it
  against the runtime's accepted range WITH THE PREPROCESSOR. Both numbers come
  from the generated config header (`nros_config_generated.h` for C,
  `nros_cpp_config_generated.h` for C++), which `nros-build-helpers` writes from
  `nros_core::codegen_version` — so neither side is authored and neither can
  drift from the Rust constant.

  WHY NOT A LINK SYMBOL. The first implementation had the runtime define one
  WEAK `nros_codegen_version_v<K>` per accepted version and had the artifact
  reference `v<G>`, so the range was a set of defined symbols and a mismatch was
  an undefined reference. It worked — measured, both languages, both boundaries
  — but it added a new weak-symbol site, and this project avoids those (see
  `check-weak-symbols` and the audited allowlist). It was also unnecessary:

    * generated code <-> the headers it compiles against  — this check;
    * headers <-> the archive it links                    — already covered by
      `nros_config_variant_<slug>` (issues 0360/0369), which is the anchor that
      exists precisely because header/archive mismatch is real here.

  Compose the two and generated-code <-> archive follows, with no second weak
  symbol. This check also fires EARLIER (compile, not link) and says both
  numbers instead of naming an undefined symbol.

  FAIL-CLOSED, AND ONE DIAGNOSIS. If the config header has not been included,
  `NROS_CODEGEN_VERSION` is undefined and `#if` would read it as 0 — so the
  range test would ALSO fire, and the reader would get two errors telling them
  two different things ("regenerate your tree" is wrong advice when the runtime
  header is the thing missing). `#elif` makes the missing-header case exclusive:
  exactly one `#error` per artifact, and it is the true one. Observed: the
  borrowed-e2e fixture emitted both before this was `#elif`.

  CARRIER. Every generated header of both languages. C has a single guaranteed
  carrier (`<nros/types.h>`, which pulls the config header) but C++ has none —
  `message.hpp` includes six `nros/*.hpp`, `service.hpp` and `action.hpp` only
  two — so the config header is included here explicitly. The per-package
  umbrella was the other candidate and is weaker: a consumer that includes only
  `<pkg>/msg/Foo.hpp` never reaches it, and a header nobody includes stamps
  nothing.

  ONE HEADER FOR BOTH LANGUAGES, and it is the C one. The range is a property of
  the codegen/runtime pair, not of a language: `nros-build-helpers` writes the
  identical pair of macros into `nros_config_generated.h` and
  `nros_cpp_config_generated.h` from the same `nros_core::codegen_version`. Using
  the C header means a C++ consumer needs one per-build directory on its include
  path rather than two — and the second is genuinely harder to get right, because
  `target/nros-cpp-generated` is SHARED and holds whatever the last `nros-cpp`
  build wrote. Reaching for it in the borrowed-e2e fixture produced an undefined
  `nros_cpp_config_variant_…_platform_zephyr` on a posix build: issue 1050's
  class, an ambient artifact standing in for a fact. Not this check's problem to
  solve, and not a problem it needs to have.
-#}
#include <nros/nros_config_generated.h>

/* RFC-0090 — emitted by nano-ros codegen version {{ codegen_version }}. */
#define NROS_EMITTED_CODEGEN_VERSION {{ codegen_version }}

#ifndef NROS_CODEGEN_VERSION
#error "nros: the generated config header did not define NROS_CODEGEN_VERSION. This generated artifact cannot tell whether the runtime accepts it; rebuild the nano-ros runtime so its config header is regenerated."
#elif NROS_EMITTED_CODEGEN_VERSION < NROS_CODEGEN_VERSION_MIN \
   || NROS_EMITTED_CODEGEN_VERSION > NROS_CODEGEN_VERSION
#error "nros: this generated tree was emitted at a codegen version the runtime does not accept (see NROS_EMITTED_CODEGEN_VERSION above against NROS_CODEGEN_VERSION_MIN..NROS_CODEGEN_VERSION in nros_config_generated.h). Regenerate it against the runtime you are building: `nros sync` in a consumer workspace."
#endif
